messages:
Maya Chen: @AcmeDev thanks for filing — I see the ticket and the failing examples. Can you paste the minimal request you used?
Customer: AcmeDev: Hey Maya, we rolled yesterday's model rollout (v2.14 -> v2.16) and our codegen tests started failing. Minimal curl we used: ```curl -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-***' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{
  "model": "redwood-2.16-code",
  "input": "Write a TypeScript function that parses a CSV into objects with typed fields and validates numeric columns. Include compile-ready code."
}'``` It used to compile on v2.14 but now there are missing imports and runtime type issues in the output.
Maya Chen: Copying in @Ben & @Nina for triage. Also adding :eyes:.
Ben Torres: thanks. @AcmeDev can you attach a failing snippet (the raw code the model returned) and the javscript/typescript compiler error lines? quick repro is best.
Customer: AcmeDev: Sure — here's the model's returned code (copied verbatim): ```export function parseCSV(data: string) {
  const rows = data.split('\n').map(r => r.split(','));
  return rows.map(cols => ({
    id: parseInt(cols[0]),
    name: cols[1],
    score: Number(cols[2].trim()),
    metadata: JSON.parse(cols[3]) // <-- but sometimes not present
  }))
}``` When we run `tsc` we get: ```error TS2339: Property 'trim' does not exist on type 'string | undefined'.``` Also sometimes the model omits `import { parse } from 'csv-parse'` lines we used before.
Nina Patel: that's helpful. Sounds like the model regressed on guard code and optional chaining. Could be a change in sampling or instruction tuning in v2.16. Do you have the original v2.14 output for comparison?
Customer: AcmeDev: Yes, attached the old output (works, has checks): ```export function parseCSV(data: string): Array<{id:number,name:string,score:number,metadata?:any}> {
  const lines = data.split(/\r?\n/).filter(Boolean);
  return lines.map(line => {
    const [id,name,score,meta] = line.split(',');
    return {
      id: Number(id),
      name: String(name),
      score: Number(score),
      metadata: meta ? JSON.parse(meta) : undefined
    };
  });
}```
Maya Chen: OK — clear behavioral delta: v2.14 adds the defensive checks, v2.16 returned more concise but unsafe code.
Rajesh Kumar: @Maya @Nina quick checklist: 1) Did the rollout change the default temperature/top_p? 2) Any model config like max_tokens/truncation affecting prompt context? 3) Was there a change to the instruction prefix or system prompt?
Maya Chen: I can confirm no runtime config changes on their side; they used same request fields. We did push a global tweak to the codegen instruction template as part of the rollout (added a shorter system prompt to reduce token length) — pinging @Ben who owns prompt templates.
Ben Torres: yep that was the change — we shortened the system prompt to shave cost for high-throughput customers. The template diff: ````- System: You are a helpful code-writing assistant. Produce complete, compile-ready solutions with type annotations and defensive checks.
+ System: You are a code assistant. Prefer concise solutions.```` Might explain missing defensive checks.
Nina Patel: that is likely the cause. We have seen instruction truncation or weaker system guidance lead to style drift. For severity: this causes compile-time failures for AcmeDev QA, so high priority.
Maya Chen: @support-bot created ticket #CS-7821 on behalf of AcmeDev. Ticket has priority=high.
support-bot: Ticket CS-7821 created; assignees: @Ben Torres, @Nina Patel.
Ben Torres: I can roll back the prompt template for the affected model class (code models) to previous wording and push a patch (no model weights change). ETA 2 hours.
Customer: AcmeDev: Appreciate that. Any chance we can get a version pin until this is resolved? We need deterministic builds.
Maya Chen: Yes — recommend switching to `model: redwood-2.14-code` in the request for now. I'll confirm the mapping and post a short how-to.
Rajesh Kumar: also capturing a reproducible automated test we can run: script that sends the input and runs `tsc`. @AcmeDev if you can share a canonical failing input file we can add to our regression suite.
Customer: AcmeDev: Attached sample CSV and a small CI job snippet. We're happy to share.
Nina Patel: triage plan: 1) rollback prompt template for code models (Ben). 2) Add a regression that runs TS compile on a set of canonical prompts (Maya + Rajesh). 3) Notify customers and recommend model pinning (Maya). 4) Add an eval metric for "compiles: yes/no" on critical corpora (Research).
Ben Torres: rolling back now. Patch includes: reintroduce explicit ask for "complete, compile-ready solutions with defensive checks and type annotations" and bump model config to include `require_compilable:true` instruction token. Pushing to prod.
Ben Torres: Deploy log: ```[2025-10-19T14:12:03Z] prompt-template-service PATCH /templates/code-default - status 200```
Maya Chen: @AcmeDev after rollback, can you re-run your test against `redwood-2.16-code`? We did not change weights — the template change was controlling output.
Customer: AcmeDev: Re-ran 10 samples just now. Results: 7/10 now include defensive checks and compile; 3 still miss optional fields. Better but not fully fixed.
Nina Patel: partial improvement suggests some cached variants or replica lag. Rajesh, can we check rollout propagation and any SLOs on template service?
Rajesh Kumar: looking — template-service has a gradual rollout with 10s ring; but logs show 200s for all replicas updated. No obvious errors. There was a stale cache at edge node in eu-west-1 which returned old shorter prompt for a while. Clearing cache now.
Rajesh Kumar: cache flush done. Also increased template-service propagation timeout to avoid partial rollouts in future.
Customer: AcmeDev: Reran after 15m — 10/10 compile-ready now. Thanks! We pinned to v2.14 for blocked builds but happy to switch back.
Maya Chen: great. Next steps: follow-up RFC to require unit-level "compilable" check for code models before prompt changes. I'll draft and assign.
Ben Torres: adding a post-mortem note: we underestimated behavioral sensitivity to concise system prompts. Will add A/B tests that include compile checks.
Nina Patel: also flag to product: any prompt change that could affect safety/compilability needs an opt-in rollout.
Customer: AcmeDev: Thanks all — appreciate fast turnaround. Leaving reaction :party:.
Maya Chen: closing loop — ticket CS-7821 will be marked resolved after a 48-hour observation window. Leaving notes in support console. :white_check_mark:
